    <h1>Comment on the air permit</h1>
    <p class="lede"><strong>The comment period closed November 9. Thank you to everyone who commented.</strong> This page is kept for the record.</p>
    <p class="lede">The New Jersey Department of Environmental Protection (NJDEP) is deciding whether to approve 12 diesel generators at 4405 Route 1. NJDEP says it will respond to every comment received on time. Your comment counts, and it doesn't need to be technical.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <div class="callout">
    <h2>How to comment</h2>
    <p data-until="2026-11-09"><strong>Comments are due by November 9, 2026.</strong></p>
    <p><a class="btn primary" href="https://dep.nj.gov/boss/permit-comment-form/" rel="noopener">NJDEP online comment form</a></p>
    <p>Or mail to: Nibras Mir, NJDEP, 401 E State Street, 2nd Floor, PO Box 420, Mail Code 401-02, Trenton, NJ 08625-0420.</p>
    <p>Either way, include this reference: <strong>"Draft Permit Comments for PN-DC1, PI 19525 PCP250001"</strong></p>
    <p class="muted small">Include your name and mailing or email address, and NJDEP will send you its final decision. Source: <a href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-10-amended-public-notice-pcp250001.pdf">amended NJDEP public notice</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>Six things to know</h2>
  <ul class="points30">
    <li><strong>No limit on emergency use.</strong> Testing is capped at 28 hours a year per engine. Emergency use isn't. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#m-hours">[details]</a></li>
    <li><strong>No one measures the pollution.</strong> The permit sets emission limits but requires no monitoring of them. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#m-monitor">[details]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Allowed on the worst days.</strong> They can run, with no hourly cap, whenever the grid operator orders an emergency voltage reduction. Grid emergencies tend to come in heat waves. Testing is banned on bad-air days; emergency use isn't. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#m-hours">[details]</a></li>
    <li><strong>The 12 generators are "Phase 1."</strong> The developer says more are planned; its representative told the Planning Board the total is 20. <a class="cite" href="https://docs.stopnjdatacenters.org/2026-03-17-russo-letter-to-township-generators-phase-1.pdf">[letter]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Up to 900 lb of smog-forming NOx an hour.</strong> With all 12 running, as much per hour as a 600 MW coal plant. They'll run far fewer hours than a coal plant, but emergency hours aren't capped. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#m-coal">[details]</a></li>
    <li><strong>"Negligible" covers testing only.</strong> Even for testing, the cancer risk is 9.86 in a million against a threshold of 10. Formaldehyde reached 3.7 times its threshold with all 12 engines running, and passes only because of a testing schedule that doesn't apply in emergencies. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#testing-only">[details]</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>What to ask NJDEP for</h2>
  <p class="muted">Pick the ones that matter to you and say them in your own words. Personal comments carry more weight than form letters; a sentence about your own home and family makes yours stand out.</p>
  <ul class="asklist">
    <li><strong>Cap all operating hours,</strong> emergencies included, in the permit.</li>
    <li><strong>Count emergency use</strong> when calculating the facility's total pollution. EPA's own guidance uses 500 hours a year as a default, not 28. <a class="cite" href="https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2015-08/documents/emgen.pdf" rel="noopener">[EPA, 1995]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Require emissions testing</strong> of the engines, and public yearly reports of how many hours they ran and why.</li>
    <li><strong>No running on bad-air days</strong> except true emergencies, and no use for grid programs or to cut the facility's electricity bills.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluate the full build-out,</strong> all 20 generators, not "Phase 1" alone. At 20 generators, the diesel cancer risk from testing alone would likely exceed New Jersey's threshold: our rough estimate is about 16 in a million, against a threshold of 10. <a class="cite" href="/south-brunswick/#m-buildout">[how we estimated]</a></li>
    <li><strong>Limit how many engines can run at once in any situation,</strong> or require exhaust controls, since all 12 together exceed the formaldehyde threshold.</li>
    <li><strong>Release NJDEP's final risk assessment,</strong> and show whether it modeled all 12 engines running at once during a long outage.</li>
    <li><strong>Disclose how much diesel will be stored</strong> on site and how many hours of operation it supports.</li>
    <li><strong>Explain why cleaner options weren't required:</strong> newer engines with exhaust controls, or cleaner, non-diesel backup for short outages.</li>
  </ul>
